Course.start({"num":12,"unit":3,"slug":"m12-compound-absolute-value","minutes":40,"calculator":"not needed","title":"Compound Inequalities and Absolute Value","subtitle":"\"And\" and \"or\" inequalities, absolute value equations, and tolerance","lede":"Sometimes a number has to be between two limits, like a part that must be close to a target size. This week: inequalities with two conditions, and absolute value as a way to measure \"how far off.\"","recap":"Solve inequalities like equations, but flip the sign when you multiply or divide by a negative. Closed circle for ≤ and ≥, open for < and >.","goals":["Write, graph and solve compound inequalities with *and* and *or*","Solve absolute value equations (two answers!)","Use absolute value to describe tolerance: how far from a target is allowed"],"context":{"title":"The telescope that was off by a hair","place":"Earth orbit · 1990","text":"The **Hubble Space Telescope** launched in **April 1990**. It cost about $1.5 billion, and astronomers expected the sharpest pictures of space ever taken. The first images came back **blurry**.\n\nThe problem was Hubble's main mirror, 2.4 meters (about 8 feet) across. It had been ground to almost the right shape, but near its edges it was too flat by about **2.2 micrometers**: roughly **1/50 the thickness of a human hair**. A testing instrument had been set up wrong, so nobody noticed until the telescope was in space.\n\nEngineers describe how precise a part must be with a **tolerance**: the actual size must be within a certain distance of the target. In math, \"within 0.01 of 5\" is written with **absolute value**:\n\n> |actual − 5| ≤ 0.01\n\nIn **December 1993**, astronauts on the Space Shuttle *Endeavour* installed corrective optics, like glasses for the telescope. Hubble has been sending back amazing pictures ever since.","ask":"Why does \"how far off\" never need to be a negative number?"},"learn":[{"title":"\"And\" inequalities: between two numbers","text":"A **compound inequality** joins two inequalities. With **and**, a number must make **both** true, so it's **between** them. {{x > -2}} and {{x <= 3}} is written {{-2 < x <= 3}}:\n\n%%nl:-2<x<=3%%\n\nTo solve one, do the same thing to **all three parts**:","worked":[{"problem":"{{-3 < 2x + 1 <= 7}}","steps":[["{{-4 < 2x <= 6}}","Subtract 1 from all three parts."],["{{-2 < x <= 3}}","Divide all three parts by 2."]],"eq":"-3 < 2x + 1 <= 7","answer":"-2 < x <= 3"}]},{"title":"\"Or\" inequalities: outside, or either one","text":"With **or**, a number needs to make **at least one** true. Often that means the two ends of the number line: {{x < -1}} or {{x >= 4}}:\n\n%%nl:x<-1 or x>=4%%\n\nSolve each part separately.","worked":[{"problem":"{{3x < -6}} or {{x - 4 > 1}}","steps":[["{{x < -2}}","Solve the first part: divide by 3."],["{{x > 5}}","Solve the second part: add 4."],["{{x < -2}} or {{x > 5}}"]],"eq":"3x < -6","answer":"x < -2"}],"check":{"q":"Which number is a solution of {{x < -1}} or {{x >= 4}}?","choices":[["{{6}}",true,"6 ≥ 4, so it makes one part true. That's enough for *or*."],["{{0}}",false,"0 is neither less than −1 nor at least 4."],["{{2}}",false,"2 doesn't satisfy either part."]]}},{"title":"Absolute value equations and inequalities","text":"{{|x|}} is the **distance** from 0. So {{|x| = 5}} has **two** solutions: {{x = 5}} or {{x = -5}}.\n\nTo solve {{|expression| = c}}: get the absolute value alone, then split into **two equations**: expression = c **or** expression = −c. (If c is negative, there's **no solution**: distance can't be negative.)\n\nFor inequalities, think distance:\n- {{|x| < 4}}: closer than 4 to zero → **between**: {{-4 < x < 4}}\n- {{|x| > 4}}: farther than 4 from zero → **outside**: {{x < -4}} or {{x > 4}}","worked":[{"problem":"{{|x - 3| = 5}}","steps":[["{{x - 3 = 5}} or {{x - 3 = -5}}","Split into two equations."],["{{x = 8}} or {{x = -2}}","Both are 5 away from 3."]],"eq":"abs(x - 3) = 5","answer":"8 or -2"},{"problem":"A bolt must be **10 mm** long, within **0.2 mm**. ({{|L - 10| <= 0.2}})","steps":[["{{-0.2 <= L - 10 <= 0.2}}","\"Within\" means between."],["{{9.8 <= L <= 10.2}}","Add 10 to all three parts."]]}]}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"quiz","title":"Graphs of compound inequalities","questions":[{"q":"Which graph shows {{-1 <= x < 3}}?","choices":[["%%nl:-1<=x<3%%",true],["%%nl:-1<x<=3%%",false,"Check which end is closed: −1 should be included."],["%%nl:x<-1 or x>=3%%",false,"That's an *or* graph: the outside."]]},{"q":"Which inequality does this show? %%nl:x<=-2 or x>1%%","choices":[["{{x <= -2}} or {{x > 1}}",true],["{{-2 <= x < 1}}",false,"That's the part in the middle, which isn't shaded."],["{{x < -2}} or {{x >= 1}}",false,"Look at which circles are open and which are closed."]]},{"q":"{{x > 2}} **and** {{x < 0}}: how many numbers work?","choices":[["none",true,"No number is both bigger than 2 and smaller than 0."],["every number",false],["just 1",false]],"keepOrder":true}]},{"id":"t2","type":"solve","title":"Solve the \"and\" inequality","pick":4,"intro":"Type answers like **-2 < x <= 3**.","items":[{"q":"{{-4 < x + 1 < 6}}","a":"-5 < x < 5","eq":"-4 < x + 1 < 6","how":["Subtract 1 from all three parts: **−5 < x < 5**"]},{"q":"{{2 <= 2x <= 10}}","a":"1 <= x <= 5","eq":"2 <= 2x <= 10","how":["Divide all parts by 2: **1 ≤ x ≤ 5**"]},{"q":"{{-7 <= 3x - 1 < 8}}","a":"-2 <= x < 3","eq":"-7 <= 3x - 1 < 8","how":["Add 1: {{-6 <= 3x < 9}}","Divide by 3: **−2 ≤ x < 3**"]},{"q":"{{0 < x/2 + 1 <= 4}}","a":"-2 < x <= 6","eq":"0 < x/2 + 1 <= 4","how":["Subtract 1: {{-1 < x/2 <= 3}}","Multiply by 2: **−2 < x ≤ 6**"]},{"q":"{{5 < 5x + 10 <= 35}}","a":"-1 < x <= 5","eq":"5 < 5x + 10 <= 35","how":["Subtract 10: {{-5 < 5x <= 25}}","Divide by 5: **−1 < x ≤ 5**"]}],"hints":["Whatever you do, do it to all three parts.","Dividing by a positive number doesn't flip anything."]},{"id":"t3","type":"solve","title":"Absolute value equations","pick":5,"intro":"Most of these have **two** answers. Type them with *or*, like **8 or -2**. If there's no solution, type **no solution**.","items":[{"q":"{{|x| = 9}}","a":"9 or -9","eq":"abs(x) = 9","how":["9 and −9 are both 9 away from 0."]},{"q":"{{|x + 2| = 6}}","a":"4 or -8","eq":"abs(x + 2) = 6","how":["{{x + 2 = 6}} or {{x + 2 = -6}}","**4 or −8**"]},{"q":"{{|x - 5| = 1}}","a":"6 or 4","eq":"abs(x - 5) = 1","how":["{{x - 5 = 1}} or {{x - 5 = -1}}","**6 or 4**"]},{"q":"{{|2x| = 10}}","a":"5 or -5","eq":"abs(2x) = 10","how":["{{2x = 10}} or {{2x = -10}}","**5 or −5**"]},{"q":"{{|x| + 4 = 7}}","a":"3 or -3","eq":"abs(x) + 4 = 7","how":["Get the absolute value alone first: {{|x| = 3}}","**3 or −3**"]},{"q":"{{|x - 1| = -4}}","a":"no solution","eq":"abs(x - 1) = -4","how":["Absolute value (a distance) can't be negative: **no solution**"]},{"q":"{{|3x - 6| = 0}}","a":"2","eq":"abs(3x - 6) = 0","how":["Only 0 is 0 away from 0: {{3x - 6 = 0}}","**x = 2** (just one answer)"]}],"hints":["Get the absolute value by itself. Then split: what's inside equals c, or equals −c.","If the absolute value equals a negative number, there's no solution."]},{"id":"t4","type":"sort","title":"Between or outside?","intro":"Is the solution a **between** (and) graph or an **outside** (or) graph?","cats":["Between","Outside","No solution"],"items":[["{{|x| < 6}}",0,"Closer than 6 to zero."],["{{|x| >= 6}}",1,"At least 6 away from zero."],["{{|x - 2| <= 3}}",0],["{{|x + 1| > 4}}",1],["{{|x| < -2}}",2,"A distance is never less than a negative number."],["{{-3 < x < 1}}",0],["{{x < 0}} or {{x > 5}}",1]],"hints":["Less than → between. Greater than → outside.","Compare a distance with a negative number: what happens?"]},{"id":"t5","type":"solve","title":"Absolute value inequalities","pick":4,"intro":"Type a between answer like **-4 < x < 4**, or an outside answer like **x < -4 or x > 4**.","items":[{"q":"{{|x| < 7}}","a":"-7 < x < 7","eq":"abs(x) < 7","how":["Less than → between: **−7 < x < 7**"]},{"q":"{{|x| >= 2}}","a":"x <= -2 or x >= 2","eq":"abs(x) >= 2","how":["Greater than → outside: **x ≤ −2 or x ≥ 2**"]},{"q":"{{|x - 3| <= 4}}","a":"-1 <= x <= 7","eq":"abs(x - 3) <= 4","how":["{{-4 <= x - 3 <= 4}}","Add 3: **−1 ≤ x ≤ 7**"]},{"q":"{{|x + 5| > 2}}","a":"x < -7 or x > -3","eq":"abs(x + 5) > 2","how":["{{x + 5 < -2}} or {{x + 5 > 2}}","**x < −7 or x > −3**"]},{"q":"{{|2x - 1| < 9}}","a":"-4 < x < 5","eq":"abs(2x - 1) < 9","how":["{{-9 < 2x - 1 < 9}}","{{-8 < 2x < 10}}, so **−4 < x < 5**"]}],"hints":["|stuff| < c means −c < stuff < c.","|stuff| > c means stuff < −c or stuff > c."]},{"id":"t6","type":"solve","title":"Tolerance","intro":"Write an absolute value inequality, then find the smallest and largest allowed values.","items":[{"q":"A cereal box should hold **500 g**, within **8 g**. What's the **smallest** allowed weight? ({{|w - 500| <= 8}})","a":"492","post":"g","how":["{{-8 <= w - 500 <= 8}}","{{492 <= w <= 508}}: smallest **492 g**"]},{"q":"Same box: what's the **largest** allowed weight?","a":"508","post":"g","how":["**508 g**"]},{"q":"A thermostat is set to **68°F** and keeps the room within **3 degrees**. Write the range of temperatures as a compound inequality in {{t}}.","a":"65 <= t <= 71","how":["{{|t - 68| <= 3}}","**65 ≤ t ≤ 71**"]},{"q":"Hubble's mirror edge was off by **2.2 micrometers**. If the tolerance had been **0.05 micrometers** (about 1/40 of the error), how many times too big was the error? ({{2.2 / 0.05}})","a":"44","post":"times","from":"2.2/0.05","how":["{{2.2/0.05 = 44}}"]}],"hints":["*Within* 8 of 500 means from 500 − 8 to 500 + 8."]}],"build":{"heading":"Design a tolerance","prompt":"Pick something that has to be close to a target size, time or amount: a basketball's air pressure, a pizza's bake time, a shoe size, a medicine dose, a track runner's pace. Write an **absolute value inequality** for its tolerance, **solve** it as a compound inequality, and explain what the two endpoints mean.","model":"A basketball should be inflated to 8 psi, within 0.5 psi. So |p - 8| ≤ 0.5. That means -0.5 ≤ p - 8 ≤ 0.5, so 7.5 ≤ p ≤ 8.5. The ball is legal if the pressure is anywhere from 7.5 psi to 8.5 psi, including the endpoints.","placeholder":"A ___ should be ___, within ___. So |…| ≤ …","reqs":[{"t":"An absolute value inequality, like |p - 8| ≤ 0.5","re":"\\|[^|]+\\|\\s*[<>≤≥]"},{"t":"A compound inequality (two inequality signs)","re":"[<>≤≥][^<>≤≥\\n]+[<>≤≥]"},{"t":"Explain the endpoints (use *from*, *between* or *endpoints*)","re":"\\b(from|between|endpoints?)\\b"},{"t":"At least 3 sentences","sentences":3}]},"reflect":{"q":"Hubble's mistake was tiny, about 1/50 of a hair, but it mattered a lot. And it was fixed, and Hubble became one of the most successful telescopes ever. Think of a mistake (yours or someone else's) that got fixed and led to something good. What did you learn from it?","placeholder":"A mistake that got fixed was…"},"challenge":[{"id":"x1","type":"solve","title":"Absolute value workouts","intro":"Type two answers with *or*. Type **no solution** or **all real numbers** if that's the answer.","items":[{"q":"{{|2x - 5| + 3 = 10}}","a":"6 or -1","eq":"abs(2x - 5) + 3 = 10","how":["{{|2x - 5| = 7}}","{{2x - 5 = 7}} or {{2x - 5 = -7}}","**6 or −1**"]},{"q":"{{-2|x + 1| = -8}}","a":"3 or -5","eq":"-2*abs(x + 1) = -8","how":["Divide by −2: {{|x + 1| = 4}}","**3 or −5**"]},{"q":"{{3|x - 4| + 2 = 2}}","a":"4","eq":"3*abs(x - 4) + 2 = 2","how":["{{|x - 4| = 0}}","Just **x = 4**"]},{"q":"{{|x + 2| >= 0}}","a":"all real numbers","how":["Every absolute value is at least 0: **all real numbers**"]}]},{"id":"x2","type":"quiz","title":"Write the absolute value","questions":[{"q":"Which absolute value inequality matches {{-3 <= x <= 7}}?","choices":[["{{|x - 2| <= 5}}",true,"The middle is 2 and each end is 5 away."],["{{|x - 5| <= 2}}",false],["{{|x + 2| <= 5}}",false,"That would be centered at −2."]]},{"q":"Which matches {{x < 1}} or {{x > 9}}?","choices":[["{{|x - 5| > 4}}",true,"Centered at 5, more than 4 away."],["{{|x - 4| > 5}}",false],["{{|x - 5| < 4}}",false,"That's the between version."]]}]}],"culture":"**Did you know?** The bars for absolute value, |x|, were introduced by Karl Weierstrass in 1841. Before that, people had to write out \"the numerical value of x.\"","unitTitle":"Inequalities and Functions","code":"Mzk5MS1FQ05BUkVMT1Q=","star":"NDkwNS1TSUdBTQ==","total":30,"prev":"m11-inequalities.html","next":"m13-coordinate-plane.html"});
